From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 21:08:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/hevc: decode the base layer of a multilayer stream
 with a separate decoder

When more than one layer is decoded (MV-HEVC), the new option
base_layer_decoder=separate has the base layer decoded by a second HEVC
decoder of its own: on the hardware device of hw_device_ctx where the
caller set one, in software otherwise. Most hardware decoders take the
base layer of an MV-HEVC stream, plain Main or Main 10 HEVC, but not the
layer above it; this one then decodes that layer in software, its
inter-layer reference being the separate decoder's picture of the same
access unit copied to memory (NV12, P010 or the decoder's own format).
It still parses all of the base layer itself (parameter sets, slice
headers, reference picture sets, POC, DPB and output) and only takes the
samples of its pictures from the other decoder: about half the work of
the processor for two views.

The pictures are those of the software decoding of every layer:
- the separate decoder gives each picture it decodes at once, in
  decoding order, and its pictures are paired by POC with this
  decoder's; anything else (no picture, another POC, an error, a
  picture that is not 4:2:0 of 8 or 10 bits) and this decoder decodes
  the base layer itself again, for good;
- its pictures are used only once the first 8 were found the same, bit
  for bit, as this decoder's own decoding of them, and 4 more are
  compared about every 480 pictures from a random access point;
- the only thing a layer above takes from its inter-layer reference
  besides its samples is the motion field, where that picture is its
  collocated picture; the separate decoder gives none, so that is
  allowed only for an all-intra base picture (whose motion field is
  known), found when the picture starts; a picture that would need it,
  or that needs the motion of one the separate decoder gave after this
  decoder took the base layer back, is marked corrupt.
Each picture says how its base layer was decoded in its metadata
(lavc.hevc.base_layer, lavc.hevc.base_layer_checks and, once the
separate decoder was given up, lavc.hevc.base_layer_error), so that a
caller can decode from the last key frame again without the option.
A picture whose base layer the separate decoder gave and that is
returned after it was given up (set up while an earlier picture whose
comparison found the fault was still being decoded, or one whose
picture could not be copied in) says so too and is marked corrupt
(AV_FRAME_FLAG_CORRUPT): this is done as it is returned, when every
frame thread that set a picture up before it has ended
(FrameDecodeData.post_process, whose reference is now always released
in decode.c). A picture that could not be copied in is mid grey of its
bit depth.

The work at the start of a picture of the base layer (giving the packet
to the separate decoder and its picture to memory) is done while the
frame thread sets the picture up, so that every fault is known before
the next thread starts; the copy into the frame and the comparisons are
done after. The value separate_semiplanar repacks the pictures of a
separate decoder in software as NV12 or P010, to test that path without
hardware. This decoder never uses a hardware decoder itself then: the
device is the separate decoder's.

Hades: tested on the host with the separate decoder in software (the
ITU-T MV-HEVC conformance streams, x265 streams of 8 and 10 bits, MP4
files, seeks, streams that start mid-GOP or lose a view, damaged
streams, 22 single-layer conformance streams, 1 to 16 frame threads,
injected faults of the separate decoder (also from a picture on, with
frames in flight, and a copy that fails), mutants of each check, ASan
and TSan): the pictures of FFmpeg without this change. The hardware
path (VAAPI) is not tested on hardware by this change.
